# CYBER
add('cyber-cia','cyber','Foundations','Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability','The CIA triad is a compact way to reason about three fundamental security properties.',
'''Confidentiality asks whether information is disclosed only to authorized parties. Integrity asks whether information and systems remain accurate, complete, and protected from unauthorized modification. Availability asks whether authorized users can access required services and data when needed.\n\nControls can support more than one property. Encryption mainly protects confidentiality in transit or at rest, but authenticated encryption also helps detect tampering. Backups and redundancy support availability and can assist integrity recovery after destructive changes.''',
'It gives teams a neutral vocabulary for tradeoffs. Security is not only secrecy; a ransomware event that destroys availability can be catastrophic even if no data is leaked.',
'A payroll file exposed publicly is a confidentiality failure. A maliciously altered payroll amount is an integrity failure. An outage on payday is an availability failure.',
'Ask which security property is at risk before choosing a control.',
minutes=7,tags=['cia triad','risk','security'],interactive='cia',related=['cyber-least-privilege'],quiz={'q':'Which property is primarily affected when authorized users cannot reach a service?','options':['Confidentiality','Integrity','Availability','Non-repudiation only'],'answer':2,'explanation':'Availability is the ability of authorized users to access required systems and data.'})
add('cyber-authn-authz','cyber','Identity','Authentication vs Authorization','Authentication establishes who or what a principal is; authorization decides what that principal may do.',
'''Authentication can use passwords, certificates, tokens, hardware keys, biometrics, or other evidence. Once identity is established, authorization evaluates policies, roles, attributes, ownership, resource context, and other rules to permit or deny actions.\n\nKeeping these concepts separate improves both design and troubleshooting. A user can authenticate successfully yet be forbidden from deleting a record. Conversely, badly designed systems can expose authorization flaws even when authentication itself is strong.''',
'Identity systems, APIs, cloud IAM, operating systems, and applications all depend on this distinction.',
'Logging into a cloud console proves identity. Whether that user may create a virtual network is an authorization decision.',
'“Who are you?” and “May you do this?” are different questions.',
minutes=7,tags=['authentication','authorization','iam'],related=['cyber-least-privilege','software-api-lifecycle'],quiz={'q':'A logged-in user receives 403 when attempting an admin operation. What most directly failed?','options':['Authorization','Authentication must have failed','DNS caching','Disk formatting'],'answer':0,'explanation':'The system recognized the user but did not allow the requested action.'})
add('cyber-least-privilege','cyber','Identity','Least Privilege as an Engineering Discipline','Least privilege means granting only the access necessary for a defined purpose, for the necessary scope and duration.',
'''The principle applies to humans, service accounts, applications, cloud roles, filesystem permissions, database users, and automation. Good implementation also considers time: standing administrator access can often become just-in-time elevation.\n\nLeast privilege is not “make permissions tiny until work breaks.” It begins with understanding responsibilities and then designing permissions to match them. Logging, periodic review, separation of duties, and emergency access complete the control.''',
'Excess permission increases blast radius. A compromised low-privilege identity should have fewer damaging options than a permanently privileged one.',
'A backup service account may need read access to protected data and write access to a backup target, but not interactive login or domain-administrator rights.',
'Privilege should be intentional, explainable, reviewable, and removable.',
minutes=8,tags=['least privilege','iam','rbac'],related=['cloud-linux-permissions','cyber-authn-authz'],quiz={'q':'Which design best matches least privilege?','options':['Give every automation account admin rights to avoid failures','Grant a service only the permissions needed for its documented task','Disable logging','Share one root account'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Least privilege scopes access to necessary actions and resources.'})
add('cyber-threat-model','cyber','Risk','Threat Modeling: Ask How the System Can Fail Securely','Threat modeling identifies assets, trust boundaries, attackers or misuse cases, plausible attack paths, and mitigations before incidents force the discussion.',
'''A practical threat model starts with architecture: what data matters, where it flows, which identities interact with it, and where trust changes. Teams then ask what could go wrong—spoofing, tampering, disclosure, privilege escalation, denial of service, insecure dependencies, or business-logic abuse.\n\nThe goal is not to imagine every theoretical attack. It is to identify credible risk early enough that design changes are still affordable.''',
'Security reviews are far more effective when they influence architecture rather than only scanning finished code.',
'If an internal API trusts any request that reaches its network segment, the trust boundary deserves scrutiny: network location alone may not be sufficient identity evidence.',
'Threat models are living engineering artifacts. Update them when architecture or assumptions change.',
minutes=10,tags=['threat modeling','risk','architecture'],interactive='threat',related=['cyber-cia','software-api-lifecycle'],quiz={'q':'When is threat modeling most valuable?','options':['Only after a breach','Early enough to influence design, then revisited as the system changes','Only for military systems','Only after deleting logs'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Early modeling can prevent expensive design flaws and should evolve with the system.'})
add('cyber-tls','cyber','Network Security','What TLS Actually Protects','TLS protects application data in transit by negotiating cryptographic parameters, authenticating endpoints—usually the server—and establishing shared session keys.',
'''During a modern TLS handshake, the client and server agree on protocol parameters and cryptographic algorithms. The server presents a certificate chain that the client validates against trusted certificate authorities and the requested hostname. Key agreement then produces secrets used for efficient symmetric encryption and integrity protection.\n\nTLS does not make an application trustworthy. It protects the channel. A vulnerable or malicious application can still send harmful or incorrect data over a perfectly secure TLS connection.''',
'It separates transport protection from application security and explains many certificate, hostname, and proxy problems.',
'If a certificate is valid but issued for a different hostname, the encrypted connection should still be rejected by a correctly validating client.',
'TLS secures the path between endpoints; it does not validate the business logic running at either endpoint.',
minutes=10,tags=['tls','https','certificate','encryption'],interactive='tls',related=['cloud-url-journey','cyber-hash-encrypt'],quiz={'q':'What is a primary purpose of the server certificate in HTTPS?','options':['Assign an IP address','Help the client authenticate the server identity','Replace HTTP routing','Increase disk capacity'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Certificate validation binds the server’s key to an authenticated identity such as the requested hostname.'})
quick_cy=[
('cyber-hash-encrypt','Foundations','Hashing vs Encryption','Encryption is designed to be reversible with a key; cryptographic hashing is designed as a one-way transformation.','Passwords should generally be stored using purpose-built password hashing or key-derivation functions with salts, not reversible encryption. Encryption is appropriate when authorized parties must later recover plaintext.','Choose a primitive based on the property you need, not because both produce unreadable-looking output.',['hashing','encryption','passwords']),
('cyber-mfa','Identity','Multi-Factor Authentication','MFA requires evidence from more than one factor category, such as knowledge, possession, or inherence.','A password plus a hardware security key combines something the user knows with something the user possesses. Two passwords are still one factor category. Phishing-resistant methods such as FIDO2/WebAuthn can reduce risks that SMS or one-time codes do not.','More prompts do not automatically mean more independent factors.',['mfa','identity','fido2']),
('cyber-zero-trust','Architecture','Zero Trust','Zero Trust is an architectural approach that avoids granting broad trust merely because a request originates from an internal network.','Access decisions should evaluate identity, device state, resource sensitivity, context, and policy while minimizing privilege and continuously reassessing trust. It is not one product and it does not mean “trust nobody emotionally.”','Network location is one signal, not an identity proof.',['zero trust','identity','architecture']),
('cyber-firewall','Network Security','What a Firewall Does','A firewall evaluates network traffic against policy and allows, rejects, or drops flows based on attributes it can inspect.','Policies may consider addresses, ports, protocols, connection state, applications, identities, or higher-layer content depending on the firewall. Good rules are specific, documented, observable, and periodically reviewed.','A firewall can reduce reachable attack surface, but it cannot fix vulnerable code that must remain reachable.',['firewall','network security']),
('cyber-cve-cvss','Risk','CVE and CVSS Are Different','CVE identifies publicly disclosed vulnerabilities; CVSS is a scoring framework for severity characteristics.','A CVE identifier helps everyone refer to the same vulnerability. A CVSS score summarizes technical severity factors, but organizations still need context such as exploitability, exposure, asset value, compensating controls, and business impact.','High CVSS does not automatically equal highest organizational priority.',['cve','cvss','vulnerability']),
]
for id,cat,title,summary,expl,take,tags in quick_cy:
 add(id,'cyber',cat,title,summary,expl,'These distinctions prevent common security design errors.',take,take,difficulty='Foundation',kind='Quick Concept',minutes=5,tags=tags)

# LEADERSHIP
add('leadership-incident-command','leadership','Incidents','Incident Leadership: Create Clarity Under Pressure','During a major incident, leadership is the work of establishing roles, priorities, communication, and decision cadence so technical responders can solve the problem.',
'''A useful incident structure separates coordination from hands-on diagnosis. One person owns overall command, technical leads investigate specific workstreams, and a communications role keeps stakeholders informed. The team states the current impact, immediate safety or containment priorities, hypotheses, actions, owners, and next update time.\n\nStrong incident leadership does not mean the commander personally knows the root cause first. It means reducing confusion, avoiding duplicated effort, recording decisions, and creating space for specialists to work.''',
'Unstructured incidents waste expert attention. Coordination quality directly affects recovery time and stakeholder confidence.',
'Instead of ten engineers independently asking “what changed?”, the incident lead assigns one change-analysis workstream, one infrastructure health workstream, and one application workstream, then synchronizes findings.',
'In an incident, clarity is a technical control.',
minutes=10,tags=['incident','leadership','operations'],interactive='incident',related=['cloud-recovery-rto-rpo','leadership-postmortem'],quiz={'q':'What is the incident commander’s primary job?','options':['Personally type every command','Coordinate priorities, ownership, communication, and decisions','Avoid documenting anything','Guarantee the first hypothesis is correct'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Incident command is coordination and decision structure, not monopolizing technical work.'})
add('leadership-delegation','leadership','Leadership','Delegation: Transfer Outcomes, Not Just Tasks','Effective delegation gives someone a clear outcome, authority boundaries, context, resources, and a feedback loop.',
'''Weak delegation sounds like “handle this” with hidden expectations. Micromanagement gives a task but withholds meaningful authority. Strong delegation defines what success looks like, why the work matters, constraints that cannot be violated, decisions the owner may make independently, and when to escalate.\n\nThe manager remains accountable for creating the conditions for success. Delegation is not abdication; it is deliberate distribution of ownership.''',
'IT managers scale through other people. If every decision returns to one person, that person becomes a reliability bottleneck.',
'“Own the switch refresh for Building A. Success means zero unplanned outage, inventory reconciled, rollback documented, and completion by Friday. You may schedule technicians and coordinate with facilities; escalate any change to the maintenance window.”',
'Delegate the outcome and decision space, not merely the labor.',
minutes=8,tags=['delegation','management','ownership'],related=['leadership-raci'],quiz={'q':'Which is strongest delegation?','options':['“Do this because I said so.”','A clear outcome, constraints, authority, and check-in points','No context and no authority','Manager redoes every decision'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Good delegation clarifies both responsibility and decision rights.'})
add('leadership-postmortem','leadership','Operations','Blameless Postmortems That Still Have Accountability','A blameless postmortem studies how the system allowed an incident to occur without pretending that individual decisions do not matter.',
'''Blameless means avoiding simplistic moral judgment when people acted within the information, incentives, tools, and pressures present at the time. The analysis reconstructs timeline, contributing conditions, detection gaps, decision context, and recovery actions.\n\nAccountability remains: action items need owners and due dates, risky behavior can require coaching, and policy violations can still be addressed. The distinction is between learning from system conditions and scapegoating a person as the entire explanation.''',
'If engineers fear punishment for reporting mistakes or near misses, the organization loses information it needs to improve reliability.',
'“Engineer X deployed bad code” is weak analysis. “The deployment system allowed an unreviewed production change, lacked automatic rollback, and alarms detected impact after 18 minutes” creates fixable work.',
'The goal is not to remove responsibility; it is to produce better explanations and better systems.',
minutes=9,tags=['postmortem','reliability','culture'],related=['leadership-incident-command'],quiz={'q':'What is a strong postmortem output?','options':['A person to blame and no system changes','Specific contributing factors plus owned corrective actions','Deleting the timeline','Avoiding difficult decisions'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Useful postmortems turn evidence into system improvements with accountable follow-through.'})
add('leadership-tech-debt','leadership','Strategy','Technical Debt Is a Portfolio, Not a Single Number','Technical debt is the future cost created when current design, maintenance, tooling, or documentation choices make later change harder or riskier.',
'''Not all debt is bad. Teams may intentionally accept a shortcut to meet a critical deadline, just as a business can take financial debt for a useful investment. The management problem is visibility and repayment strategy.\n\nPrioritize debt by operational risk, change frequency, customer impact, security exposure, engineer time, and dependency centrality. A fragile component touched every week may deserve attention before an ugly but stable internal tool.''',
'Leaders need a language for engineering maintenance that competes fairly with feature work instead of relying on “the code feels bad.”',
'A manual deployment process that causes repeated production errors has both labor cost and incident risk. Automating it may have clearer value than refactoring an isolated script nobody changes.',
'Prioritize debt by the cost of carrying it, not by aesthetic discomfort.',
minutes=8,tags=['technical debt','strategy','prioritization'],related=['leadership-tco'],quiz={'q':'Which debt item is usually easiest to justify first?','options':['The one with measurable recurring risk and cost','The one with the ugliest variable names only','Any item chosen randomly','None; debt should never be addressed'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Recurring business/operational impact makes prioritization evidence-based.'})
add('leadership-change','leadership','ITSM','Change Management Without Bureaucracy Theater','Change management reduces avoidable risk by making important changes understandable, reviewable, scheduled, observable, and reversible.',
'''Good change practice scales with risk. A routine, automated, well-tested standard change should not require the same ceremony as a high-impact database migration. The core questions are stable: what is changing, why, who owns it, what could fail, how will we validate success, how do we roll back, and who must know?\n\nExcessive process creates incentives to bypass controls. Too little process creates invisible risk. Mature organizations automate evidence and approvals where possible.''',
'Production systems fail both from bad changes and from organizations becoming too afraid to change them. The goal is safer flow, not frozen infrastructure.',
'A network-core upgrade may need a maintenance window, peer review, configuration backup, console access, validation steps, rollback image, and stakeholder notice.',
'Risk-based change control protects speed by making safe changes repeatable.',
minutes=9,tags=['change management','itsm','risk'],related=['leadership-incident-command'],quiz={'q':'Which question belongs in nearly every meaningful change plan?','options':['How will success be validated and how can we recover?','How can documentation be avoided?','How can we hide the change?','How can all changes use identical bureaucracy?'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Validation and recovery planning are core controls.'})
quick_lead=[
('leadership-raci','Projects','RACI','RACI distinguishes who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, and Informed for a work item.','Used carefully, it exposes ambiguous ownership. “Responsible” performs the work; “Accountable” owns the outcome. Too many accountable people can mean nobody truly owns the decision.','Use RACI to clarify real decisions, not to decorate every task in a project plan.',['raci','project management']),
('leadership-sli-slo-sla','Operations','SLI, SLO, SLA','An SLI is a measured service indicator; an SLO is a target for that indicator; an SLA is a formal commitment that may have business consequences.','For example, request success rate can be an SLI, 99.9% monthly success an SLO, and a customer contract may define an SLA with credits if a specified threshold is missed.','Measure first, target second, contract carefully.',['sli','slo','sla','sre']),
('leadership-one-on-one','Leadership','One-on-Ones','A useful one-on-one is protected time for the employee’s context, growth, obstacles, feedback, and relationship with the manager.','Status can be handled elsewhere. Strong one-on-ones surface problems early, develop judgment, and create a recurring channel for goals and feedback.','Do not turn every one-on-one into a ticket review.',['management','1:1','coaching']),
('leadership-psych-safety','Teams','Psychological Safety','Psychological safety is a team climate where people can raise concerns, admit uncertainty, ask questions, and report mistakes without interpersonal punishment.','It does not mean avoiding standards or difficult feedback. High-performing technical teams need both candor and accountability.','A team that hides bad news is operationally dangerous.',['teams','culture','safety']),
('leadership-tco','Finance','Total Cost of Ownership','TCO looks beyond purchase price to implementation, licensing, labor, training, support, downtime, integration, security, energy, and retirement costs.','A cheaper tool can cost more overall if it requires manual administration, creates outages, or forces expensive migrations later.','Buying decisions should compare lifecycle cost and risk, not sticker price alone.',['tco','budget','vendor']),
]
for id,cat,title,summary,expl,take,tags in quick_lead:
 add(id,'leadership',cat,title,summary,expl,'These frameworks make management decisions more explicit.',take,take,difficulty='Foundation',kind='Quick Concept',minutes=5,tags=tags)

# MATH
add('math-functions','math','Functions','Functions: Inputs, Rules, Outputs','A function associates each allowed input with exactly one output.',
'''Writing y=f(x) means “apply the rule f to x and call the result y.” The set of allowed inputs is the domain, and the set of outputs actually produced is the range. Functions can be represented by formulas, tables, graphs, code, or verbal rules.\n\nComposition connects functions: (f∘g)(x)=f(g(x)). In computing, this resembles feeding the output of one transformation into another. In engineering, functions describe relationships such as position over time, voltage versus current, or cost versus utilization.''',
'Functions are the basic language of calculus, modeling, algorithms, signal processing, and machine learning.',
'If f(x)=2x+3, then f(4)=11. The symbol f is the rule; f(4) is one evaluated result.',
'A graph is a picture of a function, not the definition of what a function is.',
minutes=7,tags=['function','algebra','domain','range'],related=['math-derivatives'],quiz={'q':'For f(x)=3x-1, what is f(5)?','options':['8','14','15','16'],'answer':1,'explanation':'3×5−1 = 14.'})
add('math-vectors','math','Linear Algebra','Vectors: Magnitude and Direction—and More','A vector is an element of a vector space; in elementary geometry it is often visualized as an ordered list with magnitude and direction.',
'''In two dimensions, v=(3,4) can be drawn as an arrow from the origin to (3,4). Its Euclidean magnitude is √(3²+4²)=5. Vector addition combines corresponding components, and scalar multiplication stretches or reverses a vector.\n\nIn linear algebra, vectors need not literally be arrows. Lists of measurements, coefficients of a polynomial, signals, and quantum state amplitudes can all live in vector spaces when they obey the required operations.''',
'Vectors provide the language for graphics, forces, optimization, machine learning, quantum mechanics, and numerical computing.',
'(1,2)+(3,−1)=(4,1). Multiplying (3,4) by 2 gives (6,8).',
'“Vector” is an algebraic role. The arrow picture is one useful representation.',
minutes=9,tags=['vectors','linear algebra','magnitude'],interactive='vector',related=['math-dot-product','advanced-qubit-superposition'],quiz={'q':'What is the magnitude of (3,4)?','options':['3','4','5','7'],'answer':2,'explanation':'√(3²+4²)=5.'})
add('math-derivatives','math','Calculus','The Derivative as Local Rate of Change','A derivative measures how rapidly a function changes with respect to its input at a point.',
'''Start with an average rate of change between two points: [f(x+h)−f(x)]/h. As h approaches zero, the secant line approaches a tangent line when the limit exists. That limiting slope is the derivative f′(x).\n\nThe derivative is simultaneously geometric (slope), physical (instantaneous rate), and algebraic (a new function). In motion, if position is s(t), velocity is ds/dt and acceleration is d²s/dt².''',
'Derivatives connect changing quantities across physics, optimization, control systems, economics, and machine learning.',
'For f(x)=x², f′(x)=2x. At x=3 the tangent slope is 6.',
'A derivative is not “divide by zero.” It is defined through a limit of ratios as the interval shrinks.',
minutes=10,tags=['calculus','derivative','slope'],interactive='derivative',related=['math-functions','physics-newton'],quiz={'q':'If f(x)=x², what is f′(4)?','options':['4','8','16','32'],'answer':1,'explanation':'The derivative is 2x, so at x=4 the slope is 8.'})
add('math-matrices','math','Linear Algebra','Matrices as Transformations','A matrix can represent a linear transformation that maps vectors to new vectors.',
'''A 2×2 matrix acts on a two-component vector through row-by-column multiplication. Different matrices can scale, rotate, reflect, shear, or combine transformations. Matrix multiplication corresponds to composition: applying B and then A is represented by AB.\n\nThis transformation view is more powerful than thinking of a matrix as merely a rectangular table. It explains why order matters: in general AB ≠ BA.''',
'Matrices appear in graphics, circuits, state-space control, statistics, neural networks, HPC, and quantum computing.',
'The identity matrix [[1,0],[0,1]] leaves every vector unchanged. A rotation matrix changes direction while preserving length.',
'Matrix multiplication encodes composition of transformations, so order usually matters.',
minutes=10,tags=['matrix','linear algebra','transformation'],interactive='matrix',related=['math-vectors','math-eigen'],quiz={'q':'Why can AB differ from BA?','options':['Matrix multiplication represents ordered composition','Numbers are random','Matrices cannot be multiplied','Because all matrices are diagonal'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Applying transformation B then A generally differs from applying A then B.'})
add('math-probability','math','Probability','Probability: Quantifying Uncertainty','Probability assigns numerical weight to possible outcomes under a defined model.',
'''For equally likely finite outcomes, probability can often be counted as favorable outcomes divided by total outcomes. More generally, probability theory defines events, distributions, conditional probability, expectation, and independence.\n\nConditional probability P(A|B) asks how the probability of A changes given that B is known. Independence means knowing B does not change the probability of A; it is a precise mathematical condition, not merely “the events seem unrelated.”''',
'Probability underlies reliability, statistics, queueing, machine learning, randomized algorithms, and quantum measurement.',
'For a fair six-sided die, P(even)=3/6=1/2. Given that the result is greater than 3, the possible outcomes are {4,5,6}, so P(even | >3)=2/3.',
'Probability statements depend on a model and information state. Always ask “conditional on what?”',
minutes=9,tags=['probability','conditional','random'],related=['math-bayes','advanced-qubit-superposition'],quiz={'q':'For a fair die, what is P(result > 4)?','options':['1/6','1/3','1/2','2/3'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Outcomes 5 and 6 are favorable: 2/6 = 1/3.'})
quick_math=[
('math-scalars','Linear Algebra','Scalar','A scalar is a single element of the underlying number system used to scale vectors or other quantities.','In ordinary real-vector spaces, scalars are real numbers. In quantum mechanics, vector spaces commonly use complex-number scalars.','A scalar has magnitude but is not a direction vector.',['scalar','linear algebra']),
('math-logarithms','Algebra','Logarithms','A logarithm answers the inverse question of exponentiation: log_b(x)=y means b^y=x.','Logs turn multiplication into addition and powers into products, which is why they appear in complexity analysis, decibel scales, information theory, exponential growth, and scientific notation.','log₂(8)=3 because 2³=8.',['logarithm','exponent']),
('math-eigen','Linear Algebra','Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors','An eigenvector is a nonzero vector whose direction is unchanged by a linear transformation; the eigenvalue tells how it is scaled.','For Av=λv, v is an eigenvector and λ is its eigenvalue. These structures reveal natural modes in dynamical systems, quantum observables, principal components, and stability analysis.','Eigenvectors are special directions of a transformation.',['eigenvalue','eigenvector','matrix']),
('math-dot-product','Linear Algebra','Dot Product','The dot product combines two vectors into a scalar and relates directly to angle and projection.','For real vectors, a·b is the sum of componentwise products. Geometrically, a·b=|a||b|cosθ. A zero dot product means nonzero vectors are orthogonal.','The dot product measures alignment as well as doing arithmetic.',['dot product','vector','projection']),
('math-bayes','Probability','Bayes’ Theorem','Bayes’ theorem updates a probability when new evidence is observed.','P(A|B)=P(B|A)P(A)/P(B). The prior P(A), likelihood P(B|A), and evidence P(B) combine to produce the posterior P(A|B). Base rates matter, especially when interpreting medical tests, alerts, or anomaly detection.','Strong evidence can still produce many false positives when the underlying event is extremely rare.',['bayes','probability','statistics']),
]
for id,cat,title,summary,expl,take,tags in quick_math:
 add(id,'math',cat,title,summary,expl,'These ideas recur throughout engineering and computing.',take,take,difficulty='Foundation',kind='Quick Concept',minutes=5,tags=tags)

# PHYSICS
add('physics-newton','physics','Mechanics','Newton’s Laws as a Model of Motion','Newton’s laws relate forces to changes in motion within classical mechanics.',
'''The first law says an object maintains constant velocity unless a net external force acts. The second law relates net force to the rate of change of momentum; for constant mass this becomes F=ma. The third law says interactions produce equal-and-opposite forces on different bodies.\n\nForces should be represented on a free-body diagram before equations are written. That discipline prevents common mistakes such as canceling a third-law pair that actually acts on two different objects.''',
'Classical mechanics is foundational to machines, structures, vehicles, robotics, and control.',
'A 10 kg cart experiencing 20 N of net horizontal force has acceleration 2 m/s².',
'Newton’s third-law forces act on different objects, so they do not cancel on one object’s free-body diagram.',
minutes=9,tags=['newton','force','acceleration'],interactive='force',related=['physics-energy','math-derivatives'],quiz={'q':'A 5 kg object has 15 N net force. Its acceleration is…','options':['3 m/s²','10 m/s²','20 m/s²','75 m/s²'],'answer':0,'explanation':'a=F/m=15/5=3 m/s².'})
add('physics-energy','physics','Energy','Work and Energy','Work transfers energy when a force acts through displacement; kinetic and potential energy provide useful bookkeeping for mechanical systems.',
'''For a constant force parallel to displacement, work is W=Fd. More generally, work is the integral of force along a path. The work–energy theorem states that net work changes kinetic energy. Potential-energy functions let conservative forces be handled through energy differences.\n\nEnergy methods can solve problems without explicitly computing every intermediate acceleration, which often makes them more efficient than force-by-force time analysis.''',
'Energy conservation connects mechanics to thermodynamics, circuits, fields, and engineering efficiency.',
'Lifting a mass increases gravitational potential energy by approximately mgh near Earth’s surface. When it falls without losses, that potential can become kinetic energy.',
'Energy is conserved in an appropriate closed-system accounting, but useful mechanical energy can transform into heat, sound, deformation, and other forms.',
minutes=9,tags=['energy','work','kinetic','potential'],related=['physics-power'],quiz={'q':'Near Earth, raising mass m by height h changes gravitational potential energy by approximately…','options':['m/h','mgh','gh/m','mv'],'answer':1,'explanation':'For roughly constant gravitational acceleration, ΔU=mgh.'})
add('physics-waves','physics','Waves','Frequency, Wavelength, and Wave Speed','For a periodic wave, speed, frequency, and wavelength are related by v=fλ.',
'''Frequency counts cycles per second and is measured in hertz. Wavelength is the spatial distance between equivalent phase points such as crest to crest. Wave speed depends on the medium and wave type. If speed is fixed, increasing frequency shortens wavelength.\n\nAmplitude describes the size of an oscillation and often relates to energy or intensity, but it is not the same as frequency. For sound, frequency strongly influences perceived pitch while amplitude influences sound pressure and perceived loudness.''',
'Waves unify acoustics, optics, radio, transmission lines, vibration, and quantum wave behavior.',
'If sound speed is 343 m/s and frequency is 343 Hz, the wavelength is 1 m.',
'Frequency and amplitude are independent properties. A louder sound does not have to have a higher pitch.',
minutes=9,tags=['wave','frequency','wavelength','sound'],interactive='wave',related=['physics-resonance'],quiz={'q':'If wave speed is constant and frequency doubles, wavelength…','options':['Doubles','Halves','Stays identical','Becomes zero'],'answer':1,'explanation':'From v=fλ, constant v means λ is inversely proportional to f.'})
add('physics-electricity','physics','Electricity','Voltage, Current, Resistance','Voltage is electric potential difference, current is the rate of charge flow, and resistance characterizes opposition to current in a component or material.',
'''For an ohmic resistor under appropriate conditions, Ohm’s law gives V=IR. Current is measured in amperes, potential difference in volts, and resistance in ohms. Electrical power can be written P=VI, which combines with Ohm’s law to produce forms such as I²R and V²/R for resistive loads.\n\nVoltage does not “flow.” Charge flows; voltage describes energy per unit charge between points.''',
'These quantities are the entry point to circuits, power systems, electronics, and computer hardware.',
'A 12 V source across a 6 Ω resistor produces 2 A in the ideal Ohm’s-law model, dissipating 24 W.',
'Current is flow of charge; voltage is a difference in electric potential.',
minutes=9,tags=['voltage','current','resistance','ohm'],interactive='ohm',related=['physics-power'],quiz={'q':'A 10 Ω resistor across 20 V carries…','options':['0.5 A','2 A','10 A','200 A'],'answer':1,'explanation':'I=V/R=20/10=2 A.'})
add('physics-quantum-measurement','physics','Quantum','Quantum Measurement: Probabilities Become Outcomes','Quantum theory predicts probabilities for measurement outcomes based on the state and the observable being measured.',
'''A quantum state can be expressed as a combination of basis states. Measurement in a chosen basis produces one of the allowed outcomes with probabilities determined by the state amplitudes through the Born rule. After an ideal projective measurement, the state is updated consistently with the observed outcome.\n\nThe measurement basis matters. A state that is definite in one basis can be probabilistic in another. This is one reason quantum information cannot be understood as a classical hidden bit with an unknown value.''',
'Measurement connects the mathematical state description to classical data that experiments and programs can record.',
'A qubit prepared in |0⟩ has a certain outcome when measured in the computational basis, but after a Hadamard rotation the same physical qubit can yield 0 or 1 probabilistically in that basis.',
'Quantum randomness is tied to the measurement context and state, not merely ignorance about a pre-existing classical value in the simple model.',
minutes=10,tags=['quantum','measurement','born rule'],related=['advanced-qubit-superposition','math-probability'],quiz={'q':'What determines measurement probabilities in the standard quantum formalism?','options':['File permissions','Squared magnitudes of relevant amplitudes','IP subnet masks','CPU temperature only'],'answer':1,'explanation':'The Born rule maps amplitudes to probabilities via squared magnitudes.'})
quick_phys=[
('physics-momentum','Mechanics','Momentum','Linear momentum is p=mv for a classical particle and is conserved in an isolated system.','Impulse changes momentum: J=Δp. Momentum conservation is especially useful for collisions where forces during impact are complicated but external impulse is small.','Momentum is a vector; direction matters.',['momentum','impulse']),
('physics-power','Energy','Power','Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.','Average power is ΔE/Δt; instantaneous mechanical power can be F·v. Electrical power is commonly P=VI.','Energy tells how much; power tells how fast.',['power','energy','watt']),
('physics-resonance','Waves','Resonance','Resonance occurs when a driven system responds strongly near one of its natural frequencies.','The exact amplitude depends on damping, drive strength, and frequency. Resonance is useful in filters and instruments but dangerous in structures and rotating machinery when uncontrolled.','Resonance is a system response, not simply “two frequencies are equal.”',['resonance','oscillation']),
('physics-entropy','Thermodynamics','Entropy','Entropy is a thermodynamic state function that helps quantify energy dispersal and the number of compatible microscopic configurations.','For an isolated system, total entropy does not spontaneously decrease. The second law gives macroscopic direction to processes such as heat flowing from hot to cold.','Entropy is more precise than the slogan “disorder always increases.”',['entropy','thermodynamics']),
('physics-photons','Modern Physics','Photons','Electromagnetic radiation exchanges energy in quanta called photons, with energy E=hf.','Higher-frequency light has higher photon energy. Intensity and photon energy are different: brighter low-frequency light can contain more photons without each photon carrying more energy.','Color/frequency controls photon energy; brightness is not the same quantity.',['photon','quantum','frequency']),
]
for id,cat,title,summary,expl,take,tags in quick_phys:
 add(id,'physics',cat,title,summary,expl,'These ideas bridge everyday physics and modern engineering.',take,take,difficulty='Foundation',kind='Quick Concept',minutes=5,tags=tags)

# Hands-on / guided items — one per domain
labs=[
('lab-dns-trace','cloud','Networking','Lab: Trace a DNS Lookup','Use nslookup, dig, or PowerShell to compare resolver answers, authoritative information, and local cache behavior.','Start with a hostname you control or a well-known public domain. Record the recursive resolver you are using. Query A/AAAA records, inspect TTL values, then query an authoritative server directly if your tool supports it. Flush only your local cache when appropriate and observe which behavior changes.','The goal is not memorizing command syntax; it is learning to separate client cache, recursive resolver, and authoritative data.','Compare `nslookup example.com`, `Resolve-DnsName example.com`, or `dig example.com` depending on your platform.','Write down the layer that produced each answer and the evidence you used.','Foundation','Lab',20,['dns','lab'], 'dns'),
('lab-parallel-thinking','advanced','Parallel Computing','Lab: Find the Parallel Part','Take a simple workload and classify each step as serial, independently parallel, synchronized, or communication-bound.','Choose a task such as image processing, log analysis, or matrix operations. Draw the data dependencies before choosing threads or a GPU. Estimate the serial fraction, then use Amdahl’s Law to calculate a best-case speedup.','Parallel programming begins with dependency analysis, not with adding more threads.','For log analysis, independent files may be parsed in parallel, while final aggregation may require synchronization or reduction.','Map the dependency graph first; hardware comes second.','Intermediate','Lab',25,['parallelism','lab'],'amdahl'),
('lab-http-devtools','software','APIs','Lab: Inspect a Browser Request','Use browser developer tools to inspect request method, status, headers, timing, response content, and dependency waterfall.','Open a public site, start the Network panel, reload, and select one request. Identify DNS/connect/TLS timing if exposed, request headers, response headers, content type, cache behavior, and initiator. Compare a document request with an image or API request.','The browser is one of the best protocol laboratories you already own.','Look for status 200 versus 304, content-type, cache-control, and timing breakdown.','Translate each field into a statement about what actually happened on the wire or at the application layer.','Foundation','Lab',20,['http','devtools','lab'],'api'),
('lab-threat-model','cyber','Risk','Lab: Threat-Model a Small Web App','Draw a tiny application, mark trust boundaries, list assets, then identify plausible threats and mitigations.','Model a browser, web application, database, and identity provider. Mark where data crosses networks or privilege boundaries. Ask how an attacker could impersonate a user, alter requests, read data, deny service, or gain privilege. Prioritize by likelihood and impact.','Threat modeling turns abstract security knowledge into architecture decisions.','A database should not accept anonymous Internet connections merely because the application itself requires login.','End with specific design changes, not a list of scary words.','Intermediate','Lab',30,['threat modeling','lab'],'threat'),
('lab-incident-tabletop','leadership','Incidents','Lab: Run a 20-Minute Incident Tabletop','Practice incident roles and decision cadence using a simulated outage with incomplete information.','Choose a scenario: authentication outage, failed network change, ransomware alert, or cloud-region dependency failure. Assign incident command, technical lead, communications, and scribe. Reveal new facts every five minutes. Require each decision to state owner, evidence, risk, and next checkpoint.','Teams build incident skill before the incident by rehearsing the coordination system.','At minute ten, reveal that the first hypothesis was wrong. Observe whether the team updates calmly or clings to sunk cost.','The exercise succeeds when communication and decision quality improve, not when the team “wins” the fictional outage.','Foundation','Lab',25,['incident','tabletop','lab'],'incident'),
('lab-vector-playground','math','Linear Algebra','Lab: Explore Vector Addition','Use the interactive vector playground to change components, magnitudes, and directions while observing addition and dot products.','Adjust two vectors and predict the result before reading the displayed sum. Try perpendicular vectors and verify that their dot product approaches zero. Scale one vector and observe how magnitude changes.','Manipulation builds intuition that symbolic notation alone often does not.','Set a=(3,0) and b=(0,4). Their sum is (3,4), whose magnitude is 5, while a·b=0.','Predict first; then use the visualization as feedback.','Foundation','Lab',15,['vector','lab'],'vector'),
('lab-wave-sound','physics','Waves','Lab: See and Hear a Wave','Change frequency and amplitude, watch the waveform, and optionally hear a safe low-volume oscillator representation.','Start at a comfortable low volume. Hold amplitude constant and increase frequency: the drawn wavelength in the fixed time window changes and the heard pitch rises. Then hold frequency constant and change amplitude: pitch remains, while the displayed amplitude and sound level change.','Using sight and sound together makes independent wave parameters easier to distinguish.','Compare 220 Hz and 440 Hz. The second frequency is double and is perceived approximately one octave higher.','Frequency changes pitch; amplitude changes strength. They are not the same control.','Foundation','Lab',15,['wave','sound','lab'],'wave'),
]
