# Learning paths
# v0.2.0 — content and experience expansion
# Five additional published learning objects per domain. These are intentionally
# compact enough to scan while still connecting concepts to operational use.

# CLOUD & SYSTEMS
add('cloud-tcp-handshake','cloud','Networking','The TCP Three-Way Handshake','TCP begins a connection by synchronizing sequence state between two endpoints with SYN, SYN-ACK, and ACK messages.',
'''A client starts by sending a SYN segment with an initial sequence number. A listening server replies with SYN-ACK, acknowledging the client sequence and contributing its own initial sequence number. The client then sends an ACK. At that point both sides have confirmed bidirectional reachability at the transport layer and established sequence-space expectations.\n\nThe handshake does not prove that an application is healthy. A successful TCP connection to port 443 only tells you that a transport connection was established to something listening there. TLS and HTTP still happen afterward.''',
'The handshake is one of the cleanest troubleshooting boundaries in networking. If SYN packets leave but no SYN-ACK returns, investigate routing, filtering, address translation, or server availability before blaming the application protocol.',
'A packet capture showing repeated client SYN retransmissions with no reply suggests the failure occurs before a TCP session is established. A completed handshake followed by an immediate TLS alert points to a later layer.',
'Use the handshake as evidence: establish which layer succeeded before investigating the next one.',
minutes=9,tags=['tcp','handshake','syn','transport','packet capture'],interactive='tcp',related=['cloud-url-journey','cyber-tls'],quiz={'q':'What does a completed TCP three-way handshake establish?','options':['The web application returned valid data','A transport connection was established between the endpoints','DNS is authoritative','The server certificate is trusted'],'answer':1,'explanation':'TCP establishes transport state. Application health and TLS validation happen later.'},objectives=['Recognize SYN, SYN-ACK, and ACK','Use handshake evidence to narrow a failure domain'])
add('cloud-default-gateway','cloud','Networking','What a Default Gateway Actually Does','A default gateway is the next-hop router a host uses when no more-specific local route matches a destination.',
'''A host first decides whether a destination is on a directly connected network using its own address and prefix. If the destination is local, the host resolves a link-layer destination and sends directly. If it is remote, the host consults its routing table. When no more-specific route exists, the default route normally points to a router on the local network.\n\nThe host still keeps the remote destination IP address in the packet. What changes on the local Ethernet segment is the destination MAC address: it is the gateway interface, not the remote server.''',
'This distinction explains a common troubleshooting pattern: local resources work, but off-subnet resources fail when the gateway is missing, wrong, or unreachable.',
'A workstation at 10.20.30.25/24 can reach 10.20.30.50 directly. To reach 10.20.40.50 it sends the frame to its configured gateway, perhaps 10.20.30.1.',
'A default gateway is a next hop for remote destinations, not the final destination of the packet.',
minutes=7,tags=['gateway','routing','subnet','arp'],related=['cloud-subnetting','cloud-arp'],quiz={'q':'For a remote IPv4 destination, what Ethernet destination does a typical host use on its local LAN?','options':['The remote server MAC address','The default gateway MAC address','The DNS server MAC address in every case','A random MAC address'],'answer':1,'explanation':'The IP destination stays remote, while the local frame is addressed to the next-hop router.'})
add('cloud-load-balancing','cloud','Cloud','Load Balancing: One Service, Many Backends','A load balancer accepts client traffic and distributes eligible requests or connections across multiple backend instances.',
'''Load balancing separates the stable service entry point from the changing set of servers that perform work. Depending on the design, a balancer may operate at Layer 4 using transport information or at Layer 7 with awareness of HTTP details such as hostnames and paths.\n\nHealth checks are essential. Sending traffic evenly to three servers is not useful if one server is unhealthy. Mature designs also consider connection draining, persistence, retry behavior, TLS termination, observability, capacity, and failure of the load-balancing tier itself.''',
'Load balancing enables horizontal scale and resilience, but it does not automatically make an application stateless or highly available. Backend dependencies can still become single points of failure.',
'An HTTP load balancer can route /api requests to one pool and /images to another, while removing an unhealthy application instance from rotation after failed health checks.',
'Availability comes from the whole dependency chain; the load balancer is one coordination point, not a magic shield.',
minutes=9,tags=['load balancer','health check','high availability','layer 7'],interactive='loadbalancer',related=['cloud-url-journey','cloud-recovery-rto-rpo'],quiz={'q':'Why are health checks important in a load-balanced service?','options':['They encrypt every request','They help avoid sending traffic to backends that cannot serve it correctly','They replace all application monitoring','They create DNS records automatically'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Health checks help the balancer decide which backends are eligible to receive traffic.'})
add('cloud-ssh-keys','cloud','Linux','SSH Keys: Authentication Without Sending a Password','Public-key SSH authentication proves possession of a private key without transmitting that private key to the server.',
'''A key pair contains a private key that should remain under the user’s control and a public key that may be placed on authorized systems. During authentication, the client demonstrates possession of the private key through cryptographic operations. The server verifies the proof using the corresponding public key.\n\nGood practice includes protecting private keys, using passphrases where appropriate, controlling authorized_keys or centralized equivalents, rotating compromised credentials, and verifying host keys so users do not silently connect to an impostor.''',
'Keys reduce reliance on reusable passwords and fit automation better, but unmanaged keys can become long-lived hidden credentials. Lifecycle management matters as much as key strength.',
'A deployment account can authenticate to a server using a dedicated key whose public half is authorized for that account while the private half stays in a protected automation secret store.',
'The private key is the credential. Treat it like one.',
minutes=8,tags=['ssh','public key','linux','authentication'],related=['cyber-pki','cyber-authn-authz'],quiz={'q':'Which part of an SSH key pair should normally remain secret?','options':['The public key','The private key','The username','The server hostname'],'answer':1,'explanation':'The private key is the secret material used to prove possession.'})
add('cloud-observability-signals','cloud','Observability','Metrics, Logs, and Traces','Metrics summarize behavior over time, logs record discrete events, and traces connect work across a request path.',
'''Metrics are efficient for trends, thresholds, and aggregation: CPU utilization, error rate, queue depth, or request latency. Logs preserve event detail and context. Distributed traces follow a request through cooperating services using related spans.\n\nThe three signals answer different questions. A metric may tell you that latency increased at 14:05. Logs may expose a timeout message. A trace may show that most of the request time was spent waiting on a database call. Correlation identifiers and consistent timestamps make the signals much more useful together.''',
'Observability reduces mean time to understand. Collecting enormous volumes of data without questions, ownership, retention strategy, or useful context is not the same as being observable.',
'An alert fires because p95 API latency exceeds the SLO threshold. A trace shows one database span dominating the request, and logs on that database reveal lock contention.',
'Instrument systems so evidence can move you from symptom to dependency to cause.',
minutes=9,tags=['observability','metrics','logs','traces','monitoring'],related=['leadership-sli-slo-sla','software-caching'],quiz={'q':'Which signal is best suited to following one request across several services?','options':['Distributed trace','Static IP address','Subnet mask','Password hash'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Traces correlate spans that represent work performed across a request path.'})

# ADVANCED COMPUTING
add('advanced-binary','advanced','Architecture','Binary: The Place-Value System Under Digital Computing','Binary represents values using powers of two, giving digital hardware a compact way to encode state with two symbols.',
'''In positional binary, each digit represents a power of two. From right to left the places are 2^0, 2^1, 2^2, and so on. The binary value 10110 therefore means 16 + 4 + 2 = 22 in decimal.\n\nBinary is not limited to integers. Hardware uses agreed encodings for signed integers, floating-point values, characters, instructions, addresses, and bit fields. Understanding the underlying bits makes masks, permissions, networking, and low-level debugging much less mysterious.''',
'Computer architecture, subnetting, file permissions, machine instructions, and many security controls eventually reduce to structured bit patterns.',
'The decimal number 13 is 1101 in binary because 8 + 4 + 1 = 13.',
'Binary is ordinary positional notation with base two instead of base ten.',
minutes=8,tags=['binary','bits','architecture','number systems'],interactive='binary',related=['advanced-boolean-logic','cloud-subnetting'],quiz={'q':'What decimal value is binary 1010?','options':['8','10','12','20'],'answer':1,'explanation':'1010₂ = 8 + 2 = 10.'})
add('advanced-boolean-logic','advanced','Architecture','Boolean Logic and Logic Gates','Boolean logic combines true/false values using operations such as AND, OR, NOT, and XOR.',
'''Digital circuits implement Boolean operations physically with transistors arranged into gates. AND is true only when both inputs are true. OR is true when either input is true. NOT inverts a value. XOR is true when the inputs differ.\n\nComplex circuits are compositions of simple logic. Adders, comparators, multiplexers, decoders, and control logic can all be described with Boolean expressions and truth tables before they become hardware structures.''',
'Boolean reasoning appears at every layer: CPU design, conditional code, access policies, search predicates, firewall rules, and database queries.',
'An XOR gate with inputs 1 and 0 produces 1; with inputs 1 and 1 it produces 0.',
'Complex digital behavior is built by composing small logical rules.',
minutes=8,tags=['boolean','logic gates','and','or','xor'],interactive='logic',related=['advanced-binary','software-big-o'],quiz={'q':'When does XOR output true?','options':['Only when both inputs are true','When the inputs differ','Only when both inputs are false','Always'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Exclusive OR is true when exactly one of two Boolean inputs is true.'})
add('advanced-cache-latency','advanced','Memory','Why the Memory Hierarchy Exists','Fast storage close to the CPU is small and expensive, while larger storage farther away is slower; the hierarchy exploits locality to bridge the gap.',
'''Registers are closest to execution units. Small CPU caches sit above larger caches, then main memory, then persistent storage. Exact latency varies dramatically by processor and system, so the important idea is relative scale rather than memorizing one universal number.\n\nCaches work because programs often reuse recently accessed data (temporal locality) and access nearby data (spatial locality). A cache miss can stall execution while data is fetched from a slower level. At scale, memory behavior can dominate arithmetic cost.''',
'Performance engineering often fails when developers count operations but ignore where data lives and how it moves.',
'A loop that walks an array sequentially can use cache lines efficiently, while a pointer-heavy traversal with poor locality may spend much more time waiting on memory.',
'Performance depends on data movement as much as computation.',
minutes=10,tags=['cache','latency','memory hierarchy','locality'],interactive='cachelatency',related=['advanced-memory-hierarchy','advanced-numa'],quiz={'q':'What principle helps caches work well for many programs?','options':['Locality of reference','DNS recursion','Password reuse','Random packet loss'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Temporal and spatial locality make recently or nearby data likely to be reused.'})
add('advanced-virtual-memory','advanced','Memory','Virtual Memory: The Address Space Illusion','Virtual memory lets each process operate in its own logical address space while hardware and the operating system map virtual pages to physical memory or other backing.',
'''A process normally issues virtual addresses. Page tables describe mappings and permissions, and the memory-management unit performs translation with help from a translation lookaside buffer (TLB). Pages can be present, absent, read-only, executable, shared, or mapped to files depending on the operating system and architecture.\n\nA page fault is not automatically an error. It is an event indicating that the current mapping cannot satisfy the access immediately; the OS may load data, create a page, reject the access, or terminate the process depending on the reason.''',
'Virtual memory provides isolation, flexible allocation, shared mappings, memory-mapped files, and a foundation for modern process security.',
'Two processes can both use virtual address 0x1000 while that address maps to different physical memory in each process.',
'Virtual addresses are process-visible names for memory; translation determines what physical resource they refer to.',
minutes=11,tags=['virtual memory','page tables','tlb','page fault'],related=['advanced-memory-hierarchy','software-stack-heap'],quiz={'q':'What hardware structure commonly caches recent virtual-to-physical translations?','options':['TLB','DNS resolver','Load balancer','RAID controller only'],'answer':0,'explanation':'The Translation Lookaside Buffer caches address translations to avoid repeated page-table walks.'})
add('advanced-process-thread','advanced','Operating Systems','Processes and Threads','A process is an execution and resource-isolation container; threads are execution flows that normally share much of a process address space and resources.',
'''Operating systems differ in details, but a useful model is that processes provide isolation boundaries while threads provide concurrent execution within those boundaries. Threads in one process can often share heap memory, open resources, and code while keeping separate stacks and scheduling state.\n\nShared memory makes communication fast, but it creates synchronization problems. Race conditions occur when correctness depends on timing between concurrent operations. Locks, atomics, message passing, immutability, and careful ownership are tools for controlling those interactions.''',
'Threads are not free speed. Work must be parallelizable, synchronization must be correct, and runtime overhead must be worth the concurrency.',
'Two threads incrementing one shared counter without synchronization can lose updates because read-modify-write sequences overlap.',
'Concurrency introduces shared-state coordination problems; parallelism is useful only when the workload and hardware can exploit it.',
minutes=10,tags=['process','thread','concurrency','race condition'],related=['advanced-amdahl','software-idempotency'],quiz={'q':'What is a common risk when multiple threads modify shared state?','options':['Race conditions','DNS TTL expiration only','Certificate renewal only','IPv4 broadcast only'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Unsynchronized shared-state changes can produce timing-dependent results.'})

# SOFTWARE ENGINEERING
add('software-queue','software','Data Structures','Queues: First In, First Out','A queue organizes work so items are removed in the same order they were added unless a different scheduling policy is deliberately introduced.',
'''The core operations are enqueue and dequeue. A simple FIFO queue is useful when arrival order should be preserved. Software systems also use bounded queues, priority queues, persistent message queues, and work queues with acknowledgement and retry behavior.\n\nQueues decouple producers from consumers. That can smooth bursts and isolate latency, but it introduces backpressure, retry, ordering, duplication, and dead-letter concerns. A queue moves the problem; it does not erase it.''',
'Queues appear in operating systems, event processing, background jobs, messaging systems, print services, and distributed architectures.',
'A web API can accept an image-processing request quickly, enqueue a job, and let worker processes consume jobs at a sustainable rate.',
'Queues trade immediate coupling for controlled asynchronous work.',
minutes=8,tags=['queue','fifo','messaging','data structures'],interactive='queue',related=['software-data-structures','software-idempotency'],quiz={'q':'What does FIFO mean?','options':['First In, First Out','Fast Input, Fast Output','File Index, File Object','Fixed Internet Flow Order'],'answer':0,'explanation':'A FIFO queue removes items in arrival order.'})
add('software-http-status','software','APIs','HTTP Status Codes as Protocol Signals','HTTP status codes classify the outcome of a request so clients can decide what happened and what to do next.',
'''The first digit groups responses: 1xx informational, 2xx successful, 3xx redirection, 4xx client-side request problems, and 5xx server-side failures. Individual codes carry more specific semantics. A 404 means the target resource was not found; a 401 means authentication is required or failed in the HTTP authentication sense; a 403 means the server understood the request but refuses it.\n\nGood APIs use status codes consistently with response bodies and retry behavior. Returning 200 for every outcome and burying failures inside custom JSON makes standard tooling and intermediaries less useful.''',
'Status codes are compact operational evidence. They help browsers, APIs, monitoring, proxies, and humans distinguish failure classes.',
'A client receiving 429 Too Many Requests should usually treat the event differently from 500 Internal Server Error and may honor Retry-After guidance when provided.',
'Use protocol semantics as part of the interface contract, not as decoration.',
minutes=8,tags=['http','status codes','api','web'],related=['software-api-lifecycle','lab-http-devtools'],quiz={'q':'Which class of HTTP status code generally represents server errors?','options':['2xx','3xx','4xx','5xx'],'answer':3,'explanation':'5xx responses indicate server-side failure conditions.'})
add('software-sql-joins','software','Databases','SQL Joins: Combining Related Rows','A join combines rows from tables according to a relationship expressed in a join condition.',
'''An INNER JOIN returns matching combinations. A LEFT JOIN preserves every row from the left side and supplies nulls where no right-side match exists. Other join forms support different inclusion rules.\n\nThe important mental model is not “join syntax” but cardinality. If one customer has many orders, joining customers to orders can produce several rows for one customer. Incorrect assumptions about uniqueness can multiply rows and silently corrupt aggregates.''',
'Joins are how normalized relational data becomes useful answers. Understanding keys and cardinality is essential to correct reporting and application logic.',
'Joining Customers to Orders on customer_id lets you list each order with customer attributes. A LEFT JOIN can also show customers who have no orders.',
'Before writing a join, state the relationship: one-to-one, one-to-many, or many-to-many.',
minutes=9,tags=['sql','join','database','cardinality'],related=['software-database-indexes','software-acid'],quiz={'q':'What does a LEFT JOIN preserve?','options':['Only rows matching on both sides','All rows from the left input, with nulls for missing right matches','Only duplicate rows','No rows without indexes'],'answer':1,'explanation':'A LEFT JOIN keeps the left-side rows even when the right side has no match.'})
add('software-caching','software','Architecture','Caching: Faster by Reusing Work','A cache stores results or data closer to where they are needed so repeated access can avoid more expensive work.',
'''Caches exist in CPUs, browsers, operating systems, DNS resolvers, CDNs, APIs, databases, and applications. Every cache needs a policy for identity, freshness, invalidation, size, eviction, and failure.\n\nThe hard part is consistency. A cached value is useful precisely because it may not require contacting the source every time, which means the cached copy can become stale. TTLs, version keys, explicit invalidation, write-through strategies, and event-driven updates are different ways to manage that tradeoff.''',
'Caching can transform latency and cost, but incorrect caching can serve stale authorization, old configuration, or inconsistent business data.',
'A product catalog cache may safely tolerate a short TTL, while an account-lockout decision should use much stricter freshness rules.',
'Every cache is a deliberate consistency tradeoff. Define how stale is acceptable.',
minutes=9,tags=['cache','ttl','performance','consistency'],related=['cloud-dns-resolution','advanced-cache-latency'],quiz={'q':'Why is cache invalidation difficult?','options':['Cached copies can become stale relative to the source of truth','Caches cannot store data','HTTP forbids caching','RAM has no latency'],'answer':0,'explanation':'The system must decide when a stored copy is no longer safe to reuse.'})
add('software-ci-cd','software','DevOps','CI and CD: Shortening the Feedback Loop','Continuous integration validates small changes frequently; continuous delivery or deployment automates the path from validated change toward releasable or released software.',
'''A CI pipeline commonly checks formatting, static analysis, tests, builds, dependency policy, and artifact creation. Continuous delivery keeps software in a deployable state, while continuous deployment automatically releases qualifying changes to production. Teams use the terms differently, so define the local meaning.\n\nAutomation improves consistency only when the pipeline itself is trustworthy. Fast feedback, deterministic builds, secure secrets, versioned configuration, observable deployment, and rollback strategy are part of the system.''',
'CI/CD reduces risky batch size and makes quality evidence repeatable, but it does not replace engineering review or production safeguards.',
'A pull request triggers unit tests and security checks, produces a versioned container image, deploys to staging, runs smoke tests, and requires an approval before production.',
'Automate the evidence and delivery path so changes are smaller, repeatable, and recoverable.',
minutes=9,tags=['ci','cd','devops','pipeline','deployment'],related=['software-testing-pyramid','leadership-change'],quiz={'q':'What is a major benefit of frequent integration?','options':['Smaller changes produce faster feedback and easier isolation of failures','It eliminates all testing','It prevents source control','It guarantees zero incidents'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Frequent small integrations reduce batch size and shorten feedback cycles.'})

# CYBERSECURITY
add('cyber-rbac','cyber','Identity','RBAC: Permissions Through Roles','Role-based access control assigns permissions to roles and then associates users or service identities with those roles.',
'''RBAC separates “who are you?” from “what job function should you be allowed to perform?” Instead of granting hundreds of individual permissions directly to every user, administrators define roles such as Help Desk, Database Reader, or Billing Administrator.\n\nThe model can still fail through role explosion, over-broad membership, stale assignments, nested-group confusion, or privileged roles that are never reviewed. Least privilege requires lifecycle governance, not merely the presence of roles.''',
'RBAC is one of the most common ways to make enterprise authorization understandable and reviewable.',
'A Help Desk role might reset standard-user passwords but not modify privileged administrator accounts. An elevated role can require separate approval or just-in-time activation.',
'Roles simplify authorization only when membership and role scope stay intentional.',
minutes=8,tags=['rbac','authorization','identity','least privilege'],interactive='rbac',related=['cyber-authn-authz','cyber-least-privilege'],quiz={'q':'What does RBAC primarily organize?','options':['Permissions around roles or job functions','DNS records around TTLs','CPU instructions around caches','Packets around VLAN tags only'],'answer':0,'explanation':'RBAC groups permissions into roles and grants those roles to identities.'})
add('cyber-pki','cyber','Identity','PKI: Trust at Scale','Public key infrastructure combines asymmetric cryptography, certificates, trusted issuers, policy, and lifecycle processes to bind identities to public keys.',
'''A certificate normally contains identity information, a public key, validity information, and a digital signature from an issuer. Clients decide whether to trust that certificate by building and validating a chain toward a trusted root under defined rules.\n\nPKI is operational infrastructure, not just math. Certificate issuance, private-key protection, renewal, revocation, hostname validation, algorithm choices, and root trust stores all matter. Expired certificates are among the simplest ways for otherwise healthy systems to fail.''',
'PKI underpins HTTPS, many VPNs, device identity, code signing, enterprise authentication, and service-to-service trust.',
'A browser validates that a website certificate is valid for the requested hostname, is within its validity period, and chains to a trusted issuer before presenting the connection as trusted.',
'A certificate is a signed identity claim tied to a public key; trust depends on validation rules and lifecycle management.',
minutes=10,tags=['pki','certificate','public key','trust'],related=['cyber-tls','cloud-ssh-keys'],quiz={'q':'What does a digital certificate commonly bind to an identity?','options':['A public key','A subnet broadcast address','A CPU cache line','A SQL join'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Certificates associate identity information with a public key and are signed by an issuer.'})
add('cyber-oauth','cyber','Identity','OAuth 2.0: Delegated Authorization','OAuth 2.0 is a framework for allowing a client to obtain limited access to a protected resource without simply handing the client a user password.',
'''OAuth defines roles and authorization flows for obtaining access tokens. The exact security properties depend on the grant type, client type, redirect handling, token validation, scopes, sender constraints, and the surrounding identity system. OpenID Connect adds an identity layer used for authentication scenarios.\n\nA common conceptual error is calling OAuth itself an authentication protocol. OAuth answers delegated authorization questions; an application that wants standardized login normally uses OpenID Connect on top of OAuth mechanisms.''',
'Modern SaaS integrations, mobile apps, APIs, and identity providers depend heavily on token-based delegated access.',
'A photo-printing service can receive permission to read selected cloud photos through scoped tokens rather than asking the user for the cloud-storage password.',
'Delegation should be limited by audience, scope, lifetime, and validation—not by sharing passwords.',
minutes=10,tags=['oauth','token','authorization','openid connect'],related=['cyber-authn-authz','cyber-mfa'],quiz={'q':'What is OAuth 2.0 primarily designed for?','options':['Delegated authorization','Disk partitioning','CPU branch prediction','DNS zone transfer'],'answer':0,'explanation':'OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access.'})
add('cyber-segmentation','cyber','Network Security','Network Segmentation: Reduce the Blast Radius','Segmentation divides networks into security zones and controls which flows are allowed between them.',
'''VLANs can create Layer-2 separation, but segmentation security depends on the routing and policy enforcement between segments. Firewalls, cloud security controls, host firewalls, microsegmentation, identity-aware proxies, and application gateways can all participate.\n\nA strong design starts from required communication paths rather than from arbitrary address ranges. Ask which systems must talk, on which protocols, in which direction, under what identity, and with what inspection or logging.''',
'Segmentation limits lateral movement, reduces accidental exposure, and gives defenders clearer policy boundaries.',
'User workstations may need HTTPS access to an internal application but no direct database access. The application tier receives only the database ports it actually requires.',
'Segment around trust and required flows, then make exceptions explicit.',
minutes=9,tags=['segmentation','firewall','zero trust','network security'],related=['cloud-vlan','cyber-firewall'],quiz={'q':'Does creating VLANs alone guarantee security segmentation?','options':['Yes, always','No; inter-segment routing and policy enforcement determine what traffic is actually allowed','Only if DNS is disabled','Only on wireless networks'],'answer':1,'explanation':'VLANs separate Layer-2 domains, but security depends on the controls governing communication between them.'})
add('cyber-vulnerability-management','cyber','Risk','Vulnerability Management Is a Lifecycle','Vulnerability management discovers exposure, validates context, prioritizes risk, drives remediation, and verifies that risk was actually reduced.',
'''Scanning is only one step. Teams need asset ownership, software inventory, severity information, exploitability context, internet exposure, business criticality, compensating controls, patch availability, exception handling, and verification.\n\nA high numeric score on an isolated test system may be less urgent than an actively exploited flaw on an internet-facing identity service. Conversely, “we patched it” is not closure until the environment confirms that the vulnerable condition is gone or meaningfully mitigated.''',
'Large environments will always have more findings than teams can fix immediately. Prioritization converts vulnerability data into risk decisions.',
'An organization can prioritize a known-exploited remote-code-execution vulnerability on public systems ahead of a higher-CVSS issue on a disconnected lab host.',
'Find, contextualize, prioritize, remediate, and verify.',
minutes=9,tags=['vulnerability','patching','cve','risk'],related=['cyber-cve-cvss','leadership-change'],quiz={'q':'Why is scanning alone not vulnerability management?','options':['Because findings still require context, ownership, remediation, and verification','Because scanners cannot find anything','Because CVEs are unrelated to software','Because patches never matter'],'answer':0,'explanation':'A lifecycle turns findings into prioritized and verified risk reduction.'})

# LEADERSHIP & IT MANAGEMENT
add('leadership-error-budget','leadership','Operations','Error Budgets: Reliability Has a Cost','An error budget converts an availability or reliability objective into an explicit allowance for imperfect service over a measurement window.',
'''If an SLO targets 99.9% availability, the remaining 0.1% is the error budget. Teams can use budget consumption to balance release velocity and reliability work. Rapid burn indicates that the service is using its reliability allowance faster than planned.\n\nError budgets are useful only when the SLI represents user experience and the organization agrees on consequences. They should not become a loophole for intentionally causing outages; they are a decision framework for risk and investment.''',
'Without an explicit reliability target, teams can argue forever between “move faster” and “never fail.” An SLO plus error budget makes the tradeoff measurable.',
'A 30-day month contains 43,200 minutes. At 99.9% availability, the simple availability error budget is about 43.2 minutes, assuming that is the chosen SLI and window.',
'Reliability targets create a budget for risk; burn rate tells you how quickly you are spending it.',
minutes=9,tags=['error budget','slo','reliability','sre'],interactive='errorbudget',related=['leadership-sli-slo-sla','cloud-observability-signals'],quiz={'q':'What does an error budget represent?','options':['The allowed amount of unreliability implied by an SLO','The annual hardware purchase budget','The number of engineers on call','The DNS cache size'],'answer':0,'explanation':'The budget is the portion of the objective window that may fail while still meeting the SLO.'})
add('leadership-prioritization','leadership','Strategy','Prioritization: Impact, Urgency, Risk, and Effort','Technical leaders prioritize by comparing expected value and risk, not by treating every request as equally urgent.',
'''Useful prioritization asks who is affected, how severe the impact is, whether timing changes the outcome, what risk accumulates if work waits, how much effort and uncertainty are involved, and which dependencies block other work.\n\nFrameworks such as impact/effort matrices, weighted scoring, cost of delay, or RICE can help, but the framework is not the decision. Leaders still need judgment, explicit assumptions, and a willingness to revisit priorities when evidence changes.''',
'IT backlogs contain incidents, compliance commitments, lifecycle work, security remediation, user requests, architecture improvements, and strategic projects competing for finite attention.',
'A failing backup restore process with regulatory and recovery impact may outrank a visible but low-risk UI improvement even when the UI request has a louder sponsor.',
'Make the criteria visible so priority becomes a reasoned decision rather than a volume contest.',
minutes=8,tags=['prioritization','strategy','risk','backlog'],related=['leadership-tech-debt','leadership-tco'],quiz={'q':'What makes prioritization more defensible?','options':['Explicit criteria and assumptions','Who sends the most messages','Always choosing the newest request','Ignoring risk'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Visible criteria let stakeholders understand why work is ordered the way it is.'})
add('leadership-vendor','leadership','Vendors','Vendor Evaluation Beyond the Demo','A vendor decision should evaluate fit, operational burden, security, integration, support, lifecycle cost, exit risk, and contract terms—not just feature screenshots.',
'''A structured evaluation starts with requirements and use cases before product demonstrations. Technical proof-of-concept work should test integration, failure behavior, administration, observability, performance, and the work required from internal teams.\n\nCommercial review should consider licensing model, renewal exposure, support levels, implementation services, data ownership, service commitments, termination terms, and migration or export paths. The cost of leaving can matter as much as the cost of entering.''',
'Poor vendor choices create years of hidden labor, lock-in, integration complexity, and operational risk.',
'Two tools may have similar subscription prices, but one requires a dedicated administrator and proprietary data migration while the other integrates with existing identity and automation.',
'Buy the lifecycle, not the demo.',
minutes=9,tags=['vendor','procurement','tco','evaluation'],related=['leadership-tco','leadership-prioritization'],quiz={'q':'Which factor is often missed in vendor evaluation?','options':['Exit and migration cost','The product name','Whether the demo has animations','The salesperson title'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Lock-in and migration effort can dominate long-term cost and risk.'})
add('leadership-feedback','leadership','Leadership','Feedback That Changes Behavior','Useful feedback is specific about observed behavior, explains impact, invites context, and agrees on what should happen next.',
'''Vague praise and vague criticism are both hard to act on. “Be more proactive” leaves the recipient guessing. A better conversation identifies a concrete situation, the behavior that occurred, the observed impact, and the expectation or experiment for the future.\n\nFeedback is also bidirectional. Managers need to ask what conditions, unclear goals, missing tools, workload, or their own behavior contributed. Accountability and curiosity can coexist.''',
'Technical teams depend on fast learning. Avoided feedback allows small misunderstandings to become performance, reliability, or relationship problems.',
'“During yesterday’s incident update, the ETA was stated as certain even though the team had not validated the fix. That caused stakeholders to plan around a promise we could not support. Next time, state the confidence level and next checkpoint.”',
'Describe reality clearly enough that the other person can act on it.',
minutes=8,tags=['feedback','coaching','management','communication'],related=['leadership-one-on-one','leadership-psych-safety'],quiz={'q':'Which feedback is most actionable?','options':['“Do better.”','A specific behavior, its impact, and a clear next expectation','Silence','A personality label'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Specific observable behavior and impact give the recipient something concrete to change.'})
add('leadership-runbook','leadership','Operations','Runbooks: Make Repeated Operations Safer','A runbook documents a repeatable operational procedure with prerequisites, decision points, validation, escalation, and recovery steps.',
'''A good runbook is not a wall of commands. It tells the operator when the procedure applies, what access and evidence are required, what normal output looks like, which steps are destructive, how to validate success, and when to stop and escalate.\n\nRunbooks should be tested during normal operations. Procedures written only after an outage and never exercised tend to rot. Where steps are deterministic and frequent, automation can execute them while the runbook explains intent and exception handling.''',
'Runbooks reduce dependence on tribal knowledge and lower cognitive load during stressful events.',
'A failed-service runbook can begin with health checks and log collection, distinguish safe restart conditions from escalation conditions, and end with service validation and incident notes.',
'Operational documentation should help a competent person make the next safe decision.',
minutes=8,tags=['runbook','operations','documentation','incident'],related=['leadership-incident-command','leadership-change'],quiz={'q':'What belongs in a strong runbook?','options':['Validation and stop/escalation conditions','Only unexplained commands','No prerequisites','No recovery guidance'],'answer':0,'explanation':'A runbook should help the operator know when to proceed, validate, stop, or escalate.'})

# MATHEMATICS
add('math-complex','math','Algebra','Complex Numbers: A Real and an Imaginary Component','A complex number has the form a + bi, where i² = -1 and a and b are real numbers.',
'''Complex numbers extend the real number line into a plane. The horizontal axis represents the real component and the vertical axis the imaginary component. A complex number can also be described by magnitude and angle, which makes multiplication closely related to rotation and scaling.\n\nEuler’s formula connects complex exponentials to trigonometry: e^(iθ)=cosθ+i sinθ. This relationship is central to signal processing, control systems, electrical engineering, Fourier analysis, and quantum mechanics.''',
'Complex amplitudes are fundamental in quantum computing, while AC circuits and signal analysis use complex representations to simplify phase relationships.',
'The magnitude of 3+4i is 5 because sqrt(3²+4²)=5.',
'Complex numbers add a second dimension to ordinary real-number arithmetic and make rotation/phase algebra natural.',
minutes=10,tags=['complex numbers','imaginary','euler','quantum'],related=['math-vectors','advanced-qubit-superposition'],quiz={'q':'What is the magnitude of 3 + 4i?','options':['3','4','5','7'],'answer':2,'explanation':'Magnitude is sqrt(3²+4²)=5.'})
add('math-rate-change','math','Calculus','Rate of Change: From Average to Instantaneous','A rate of change compares how much one quantity changes relative to another; calculus refines this into an instantaneous rate through a limit.',
'''For two points, average rate of change is Δy/Δx. On a graph this is the slope of a secant line. To describe the rate at one point on a smooth function, bring the two points closer together and examine the limiting slope. That limit is the derivative when it exists.\n\nThe concept appears far beyond geometry: velocity is position change per time, acceleration is velocity change per time, marginal cost is cost change per unit, and network throughput can be described as data transferred per time.''',
'Derivatives become much easier when they are understood as a precise language for changing quantities rather than a collection of symbolic rules.',
'If position changes from 10 m to 34 m over 6 s, the average velocity over that interval is 4 m/s.',
'Before differentiating symbolically, ask what quantity is changing with respect to what.',
minutes=8,tags=['rate of change','slope','derivative','calculus'],interactive='derivative',related=['math-derivatives','physics-newton'],quiz={'q':'Average rate of change is calculated as…','options':['Δy/Δx','x/y only','x²+y²','1/0'],'answer':0,'explanation':'It is the change in output divided by the change in input over an interval.'})
add('math-integral','math','Calculus','Integrals: Accumulating Small Contributions','An integral accumulates a quantity across an interval and can be understood as the limit of increasingly fine sums.',
'''For area intuition, divide an interval into small widths, approximate the function over each width, multiply height by width, and add the pieces. Refining the partition leads to the definite integral under suitable conditions.\n\nIntegration is not only “area under a curve.” It accumulates density into mass, power into energy, velocity into displacement, probability density into probability, and many other rates or densities into totals. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus connects differentiation and integration.''',
'Engineering constantly moves between local rates and accumulated totals.',
'If a device draws constant 100 W for 3 hours, integrating power over time gives 300 Wh of energy.',
'An integral is a disciplined way to add infinitely many small contributions.',
minutes=10,tags=['integral','calculus','accumulation','area'],related=['math-derivatives','physics-energy'],quiz={'q':'If power is constant at 100 W for 3 hours, energy used is…','options':['33.3 Wh','100 Wh','300 Wh','300 W/h'],'answer':2,'explanation':'Energy is power multiplied by time for a constant-power interval: 100 W × 3 h = 300 Wh.'})
add('math-sets-logic','math','Discrete Math','Sets, Membership, and Logical Conditions','A set is a collection of distinct elements; set operations formalize inclusion, overlap, exclusion, and relationships between collections.',
'''If x belongs to set A, we write x ∈ A. The union A∪B contains elements in either set. The intersection A∩B contains elements in both. The difference A\\B contains elements in A that are not in B. Complements are defined relative to a universe of discourse.\n\nSet reasoning connects directly to database queries, access groups, probability events, search filters, and program predicates. De Morgan’s laws reveal how complements interact with union and intersection.''',
'Discrete mathematics gives precise language to the membership questions that appear throughout software and security.',
'If Admins={A,B} and OnCall={B,C}, then Admins∩OnCall={B}.',
'Set operations are a mathematical vocabulary for “which things belong where?”',
minutes=8,tags=['sets','logic','union','intersection','discrete math'],related=['cyber-rbac','software-sql-joins'],quiz={'q':'What does A ∩ B contain?','options':['Elements in A or B','Elements common to both A and B','Elements in neither set','Only the first element of A'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Intersection contains elements that belong to both sets.'})
add('math-linear-systems','math','Linear Algebra','Systems of Linear Equations','A system of linear equations asks for values that satisfy several linear constraints at the same time.',
'''A system can be represented as Ax=b, where A is a matrix of coefficients, x is the vector of unknowns, and b is the result vector. Row reduction, factorization methods, or specialized numerical solvers can find solutions when they exist.\n\nThe geometry depends on dimension: two equations in two unknowns describe lines whose intersection may be one point, no point, or infinitely many points. Rank and linear dependence generalize those possibilities.''',
'Linear systems appear in circuit analysis, simulation, optimization, graphics, statistics, control, machine learning, and scientific computing.',
'The equations x+y=5 and x−y=1 have the solution x=3, y=2.',
'A linear system is several constraints solved together; matrices make the structure explicit.',
minutes=10,tags=['linear systems','matrix','algebra','linear algebra'],related=['math-matrices','math-vectors'],quiz={'q':'What compact form represents a linear system?','options':['Ax=b','E=mc² only','V=IR only','p=mv only'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Matrix notation Ax=b represents coefficients, unknowns, and results.'})

# PHYSICS
add('physics-kinematics','physics','Mechanics','Kinematics: Describing Motion Before Explaining It','Kinematics describes position, velocity, and acceleration without first asking which forces caused the motion.',
'''Position locates an object relative to a coordinate system. Velocity is the rate of change of position and includes direction. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. For constant acceleration, equations such as v=v0+at and x=x0+v0t+½at² connect the quantities.\n\nReal motion problems begin by defining axes and signs. A negative velocity does not automatically mean slowing down, and a positive acceleration does not automatically mean speeding up. The relationship between velocity and acceleration directions determines that.''',
'Kinematics is the descriptive layer under mechanics, robotics, navigation, control, and simulation.',
'An object starting at rest with constant acceleration 2 m/s² reaches 10 m/s after 5 s and moves 25 m during that interval.',
'Define the coordinate system first; signs describe direction, not goodness or badness.',
minutes=10,tags=['kinematics','velocity','acceleration','motion'],interactive='projectile',related=['physics-newton','math-rate-change'],quiz={'q':'Acceleration is the rate of change of…','options':['Mass','Velocity','Temperature only','Charge only'],'answer':1,'explanation':'Acceleration measures how velocity changes with time.'})
add('physics-doppler','physics','Waves','The Doppler Effect','Relative motion between a wave source, observer, and medium can change the observed frequency.',
'''For sound in air, motion changes the spacing or arrival rate of wavefronts. Approaching motion generally raises the observed frequency; receding motion lowers it. The exact equation depends on whether the source, observer, or both move relative to the medium.\n\nLight also exhibits Doppler shift, but electromagnetic waves do not require a material medium and relativistic treatment becomes important at high relative speeds.''',
'Doppler measurements are used in radar, astronomy, medical ultrasound, speed detection, flow measurement, and everyday perception of moving sirens.',
'An ambulance siren sounds higher in pitch while approaching and lower after it passes and recedes.',
'The source does not need to change its emitted frequency for the observer to measure a different frequency.',
minutes=9,tags=['doppler','frequency','waves','sound'],interactive='doppler',related=['physics-waves','physics-resonance'],quiz={'q':'For ordinary sound, an approaching source is generally heard at…','options':['A higher frequency','A lower frequency','Exactly zero frequency','No possible frequency'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Approach compresses the arrival spacing of wavefronts and raises observed frequency.'})
add('physics-electric-field','physics','Electricity','Electric Fields: Force Per Unit Charge','An electric field describes the force a positive test charge would experience per unit charge at a point in space.',
'''The electric field E is defined conceptually by F=qE for a charge q placed in the field, provided the test charge does not significantly disturb the source configuration. Field direction is the direction of force on a positive test charge.\n\nFor a point charge, field magnitude falls with the square of distance. Fields from multiple sources add vectorially through superposition. Electric potential is related but distinct: it is energy per unit charge and is a scalar quantity.''',
'Fields provide a way to reason about interactions without imagining charges “reaching across empty space” directly in every calculation.',
'A positive source charge creates an electric field pointing outward; a negative source charge creates one pointing inward.',
'Force depends on both the field and the charge placed in it; the field describes the environment before that test charge is chosen.',
minutes=9,tags=['electric field','charge','coulomb','electricity'],related=['physics-electricity','math-vectors'],quiz={'q':'Electric field direction is defined using the force on what kind of test charge?','options':['Positive test charge','Negative test charge only','Neutron only','Any mass with no charge'],'answer':0,'explanation':'By convention, field direction follows the force on a positive test charge.'})
add('physics-heat-temp','physics','Thermodynamics','Temperature Is Not Heat','Temperature characterizes thermal state; heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference.',
'''Two objects can have the same temperature while containing different total internal energies because amount and type of material matter. Heat is a transfer process, not a substance stored inside an object. When energy transfer changes temperature without a phase change, a simple model is Q=mcΔT, where c is specific heat capacity.\n\nThermal equilibrium occurs when systems in contact have no net heat transfer between them because their temperatures are equal under the relevant conditions.''',
'Confusing heat, temperature, and internal energy causes errors in cooling, electronics, HVAC, thermodynamics, and material calculations.',
'A bathtub of warm water can contain far more internal thermal energy than a tiny cup of hotter water even though the cup has a higher temperature.',
'Temperature is a state variable; heat is energy in transit because of a temperature difference.',
minutes=8,tags=['temperature','heat','thermodynamics','specific heat'],related=['physics-entropy','physics-energy'],quiz={'q':'What is heat in thermodynamics?','options':['Energy transferred because of a temperature difference','Another word for temperature','A unit of mass','A kind of electric charge'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Heat refers to energy transfer driven by a temperature difference.'})
add('physics-special-relativity','physics','Modern Physics','Special Relativity: Space and Time Depend on Motion','Special relativity describes physics in inertial frames when the speed of light is invariant and the laws of physics take the same form for all inertial observers.',
'''Einstein’s postulates lead to consequences that are small at everyday speeds but dramatic near light speed: time intervals and lengths depend on relative motion, simultaneity is frame-dependent, and energy and momentum follow relativistic relationships.\n\nThese effects are not optical illusions. They are consistent transformations between measurements made by different inertial observers. No massive object is accelerated through the speed of light in the theory.''',
'Relativity is required for high-energy physics and contributes to technologies such as satellite navigation, where relativistic timing effects must be accounted for along with general-relativistic effects.',
'Two observers in relative motion can disagree about whether distant events were simultaneous while still agreeing on the spacetime interval structure required by relativity.',
'Space and time measurements are frame-dependent, but the theory preserves consistent physical laws between inertial observers.',
minutes=11,tags=['relativity','speed of light','time dilation','modern physics'],related=['physics-photons','math-functions'],quiz={'q':'In special relativity, the speed of light in vacuum is…','options':['Invariant for inertial observers','Different for every inertial observer in the simple theory','Zero','Dependent only on object mass'],'answer':0,'explanation':'Invariant light speed is one of the foundational postulates of special relativity.'})

